---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A3: Explain how your code avoids a race if two processes attempt to
>> extend a file at the same time.

when concurrent files extense, the Pintos file system use per-inode 
locks to avoid race condition. Each inode has a dedicated lock. this 
will be initialized when opening or creating the inode. the lock will
acquired before updating file size or writing data. it also released 
after completing operation. this ensures that only one process can modify 
the file size at a time. without the lock, multiple processes could 
simultaneously extend the same file. this will cause file corruption 
or inconsistent metadata. holding the lock during the critical 
section, the system is atomicity and consistency. per-inode 
locking can enbale concurrent access to different files. this metjodeh is 
efficienter than using a global file system lock. it reducing contention 
and enabling safe, simultaneous file operations.

