---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B4: How do you prevent races on directory entries?  For example,
>> only one of two simultaneous attempts to remove a single file
>> should succeed, as should only one of two simultaneous attempts to
>> create a file with the same name, and so on.

the file system uses per-inode locks to synchronize access to avoid race. 
when a process try to create, remove, or modify a file in a directory, 
it first acquires the inode lock associated with that directory. and 
lock make sure that no other process can concurrently modify the same 
directory entry.

for eamople, when two processes simultaneously create a file with the 
same name. the first process acquire the lock succeeds and the other waits. 
also, when removing a file, the lock keep only one process can proceed, 
prevent simultaneous deletions. when completed opretaion, the lock released
and other waiting processes to proceed.

employing per-inode locking during critical operations, the system ensures 
atomicity and consistency. inthis way it can effectively preven
race conditions on directory entries.

>> B5: Does your implementation allow a directory to be removed if it
>> is open by a process or if it is in use as a process's current
>> working directory?  If so, what happens to that process's future
>> file system operations?  If not, how do you prevent it?

no. this is achieved by tracking the open count of inodes. when 
a process opens a directory or sets it as the working directory,
then incremented inode’s open count. Before removing a directory, 
the system checks this count. if the count indicates that the 
directory in use, the removal is denied.

this mechanism ensure file system operations for process continue 
without disruption. maintaining reference counts and checking them 
before deletion, the file system preserves stability.

